window.bookSummaryJSON = "<p>They told me that my wolf wasn't real. They said he wasn't my hero. But they also said the monsters were all in my head. So why do I still have the scars on my skin?</p> <p>I can barely handle being around people. I can't talk. But when he walks into my family's coffee house, my entire world changes. My brothers tell me that he’s dangerous and I should stay away from him. But he’s the only one who makes me feel safe.</p> <p>Kale Joeh was taken from her family and kept for twenty-five months and three days. When she returned, she wasn’t the same innocent, young girl. Now she can’t speak. She can’t handle strangers. She can’t be around well-dressed men without having a panic attack.</p> <p>Years later, she’s finally getting herself back to a new normal. Working in her family’s coffee house and bi-weekly therapy appointments have gotten her mostly function. Aside from the fact that she still can’t talk. So, when the tall, handsome biker walks into the coffee house late one night, Kale is surprised to find that all her fears ease. His amber eyes remind her of someone, or something, that once made her feel safe.</p> <p>Blane Gibson recognized Kale the moment he walked into the shop. He had lost track of her over the years, and to see the beauty before him was everything. He can still see the fear linger in her eyes. He swears that if she’ll give him the chances, he’ll protect her until the end of time.</p> <p>But Blane isn’t the only one from her past who’s spotted the girl. Her monsters are closing in. Can Blane and his pack protect the girl they once saved? Or will Blane lose his mate once again?</p>"; 
